{"op":"add","path":"/elements/step3-insight","value":{"type":"TextBlock","props":{"content":"Step 2 surfaced 6 high-churn months above the median threshold of 30.5705; Step 3 tests what drives them. The radar chart reveals a striking asymmetry: high-churn months score in the 78–89th percentile for new MRR, expansion MRR, customers, and churn per customer, but only the 20th percentile for ARPU — while low-churn months invert the pattern, scoring high on ARPU (69th percentile) and low on volume. This rules out a customer-quality explanation: churn rises not because worse customers join, but because faster growth periods import more customers who subsequently leave, pointing to an onboarding or time-to-value problem. (Steps 2, 3)","variant":"insight"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/conclusion","value":{"type":"TextBlock","props":{"content":"The investigation points to two compounding drivers: plan type (with Enterprise generating disproportionate churn per customer) and a growth-amplification effect where high-acquisition months also produce the highest absolute churn. The clearest next steps are to audit onboarding for the Enterprise segment and to examine whether customers acquired during peak-growth months (2024-12 and surrounding window) have materially lower 90-day retention than those acquired in quieter periods.","variant":"body"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/__synthesis","value":{"summary":"Churn intensity differs markedly by plan: Enterprise carries the heaviest burden while Pro carries the lightest, against an overall average of 30.22 per customer (Step 1). Monthly normalized churn is volatile — a 1.28× swing from trough (2024-01) to peak (2024-12) — and the trend direction is increasing, with H2 averaging 15.4% different from H1 (Step 2). Crucially, high-churn months are not explained by a lower-ARPU customer mix; they coincide with elevated new MRR, expansion MRR, and customer volume — indicating churn amplifies alongside growth rather than reflecting pricing pressure (Step 3).","conclusion":"The investigation points to two compounding drivers: plan type (with Enterprise generating disproportionate churn per customer) and a growth-amplification effect where high-acquisition months also produce the highest absolute churn. The clearest next steps are to audit onboarding for the Enterprise segment and to examine whether customers acquired during peak-growth months (2024-12 and surrounding window) have materially lower 90-day retention than those acquired in quieter periods."}}
